  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 153 PM EDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nCold frontal boundary currently draped from southwest to\nnortheast across the northwestern portions of the CWA. Current\nradar imagery, 2PM EDT, does show some scattered rain showers \nalong it's spine and look for some increase in activity as the\nafternoon goes on and the front moves southeastward. By this\nevening the rain showers should become less likely as the\ndiurnal surface heating decreases. Not much in the form of\nrainfall amounts is expected but a quick trace to a hundredth \nor so will be the most likely outcome where the showers do\noccur. \n\nFriday will be mostly sunny to partly cloudy and cooler with a\nmore northerly surface flow in place behind the front and weak\nsynoptic ridging pushing through before a weak trough skirts the\nwestern portions of the CWA on Saturday which may bring a few\nrain showers to the area. Still thinking that any showers will\nbe light and should not really make a big impact on the\nHalloween activities. A few showers look to linger into Sunday\nbut again amounts look very light especially with these\ndisturbances needing to moisten up the surface layer before any\nprecip can be realized at the surface. \n\nThe more impactful weather looks to occur next week, there is\nstill quite a bit of disagreement in the exact evolution and the\nsynoptic pattern will be fairly complicated with a split stream\nwith a southern and northern circulation. It does still appear\nthat both systems will phase late Wednesday through Thursday and\nthis looks to combine and develop a fairly deep trough\ncirculation over the Great Lakes region bringing much cooler air\nassociated with a strong cold front that looks to increase\nchances of convection with possibly a strong to severe outcome.\nThis far out though and with the guidance still at odds this is\nnot a sure outcome but certainly something to watch in the\ncoming days when more details can be sorted out.",
